    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running into a major issue with some of my dark chocolate batches. I've been seeing this weird white, dull streaking on the surface—fat bloom, I think. Can you explain the actual science behind why this happens and how I can fix my tempering process to prevent it? I'm at a high-end shop so I need to get this dialed in ASAP.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white streaking you're seeing is indeed fat bloom, a common issue in chocolate making caused by the separation of cocoa butter crystals. When chocolate is not tempered properly, the cocoa butter can crystallize in a disordered structure, resulting in a dull, streaky appearance. To prevent fat bloom, focus on developing a consistent tempering process that involves heating and cooling the chocolate to a specific range (around 105°F to 115°F) to swirl the cocoa butter crystals into a stable supercritical crystal structure. This process can become more challenging at high end, but practice and attention to temperature control can help achieve desired results.",
      "source": "target",
